ctive Journal of Medical Research 595 articles†www.i-jmr.org】\nL35:     * 【21†iProceedings 510 articles†www.iproc.org】\nL36:     * 【22†JMIR Rehabilitation and Assistive Technologies 448 articles†rehab.jmir.org】\nL37:     * 【23†JMIR Dermatology 409 articles†derma.jmir.org】\nL38:     * 【24†JMIR AI 406 articles†ai.jmir.org】\nL39:     * 【25†JMIR Diabetes 379 articles†diabetes.jmir.org】\nL40:     * 【26†JMIR Cardio 302 articles†cardio.jmir.org】\nL41:     * 【27†JMIR Infodemiology 278 articles†infodemiology.jmir.org】\nL42:     * 【28†JMIR Nursing 241 articles†nursing.jmir.org】\nL43:     * 【29†Journal of Participatory Medicine 195 articles†jopm.jmir.org】\nL44:     * 【30†JMIR Perioperative Medicine 160 articles†periop.jmir.org】\nL45:     * 【31†JMIR Biomedical Engineering 118 articles†biomedeng.jmir.org】\nL46:     * 【32†JMIR Bioinformatics and Biotechnology 85 articles†bioinform.jmir.org】\nL47:     * 【33†Asian/Pacific Island Nursing Journal 69 articles†apinj.jmir.org】\nL48:     * 【34†JMIR XR and Spatial Computing (JMXR) 62 articles†xr.jmir.org】\nL49:     * 【35†JMIRx Bio 52 articles†xbio.jmir.org】\nL50:     * 【36†JMIR Neurotechnology 38 articles†neuro.jmir.org】\nL51:     * 【37†Medicine 2.0 26 articles†www.medicine20.com】\nL52:     * 【38†JMIR Data†data.jmir.org】\nL53:     * 【39†JMIR Metascience and Research Integrity†metascience.jmir.org】\nL54:     * 【40†JMIR Challenges†challenges.jmir.org】\nL55:     * 【41†JMIR Preprints†preprints.jmir.org】\nL56:     * 【42†JMIR Reviews in Digital Health†reviews.jmir.org】\nL57: ## This paper is in the following e-collection/theme issue:\nL58: \nL59: 【43†Tools and Questionnaires in Human Factors Evaluation (111)】 【44†Research Instruments, Questionnaires, and Tools (1244)】 【45†Design, Usability, and Evaluation of Research Instruments, Scales, and Measures (161)】\nL60: \nL61: Published on 16.Dec.2020 in 【46†Vol 7, No 4 (2020): Oct-Dec】\nL62: \nL63: Preprints (earlier versions) of this paper are available at 【47†https://preprints.jmir.org/preprint/21161†preprints.jmir.org】, first published 07.Jun.2020.\nL64: 【48†Image: Spanish Version of the System Usability Scale for the Assessment of Electronic Tools: Development and Validation†asset.jmir.pub】\nL65: # Spanish Version of the System Usability Scale for the Assessment of Electronic Tools: Development and Validation\nL66: ## Spanish Version of the System Usability Scale for the Assessment of Electronic Tools: Development and Validation\nL67: \nL68: Authors of this article:\nL69: \nL70: 【49†Magdalena Del Rocio Sevilla-Gonzalez^{1, 2, 3}】 ;   【50†Lizbeth Moreno Loaeza^{3}】 ;   【51†Laura Sofia Lazaro-Carrera^{3}】 ;   【52†Brigette Bourguet Ramirez^{3}】 ;   【53†Anabel Vázquez Rodríguez^{4}】 ;   【54†María Luisa Peralta-Pedrero^{5}】 ;   【55†Paloma Almeda-Valdes^{3, 6}】\nL71: \nL72: 【56†Article】 【57†Authors】 【58†Cited by (116)】 【59†Tweetations (2)】 【60†Metrics】\nL73: #### Original Paper\nL74: \nL75:   * 【61†Magdalena Del Rocio Sevilla-Gonzalez^{1,}^{2,}^{3}, MSc, PhD】【62† †orcid.org】;\nL76:   * 【63†Lizbeth Moreno Loaeza^{3}, MSc】【64† †orcid.org】;\nL77:   * 【65†Laura Sofia Lazaro-Carrera^{3}, BSc】【66† †orcid.org】;\nL78:   * 【67†Brigette Bourguet Ramirez^{3}, BSc】【68† †orcid.org】;\nL79:   * 【69†Anabel Vázquez Rodríguez^{4}, MSc, PhD】【70† †orcid.org】;\nL80:   * 【71†María Luisa Peralta-Pedrero^{5}, MD, MSc】【72† †orcid.org】;\nL81:   * 【73†Paloma Almeda-Valdes^{3,}^{6}, MD, MSc, PhD】【74† †orcid.org】\nL82: ^{1}Clinical and Translational Epidemiology Unit, Massachusetts General Hospital, Boston, MA, United States\nL83: \nL84: ^{2}Department of Medicine, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA, United States\nL85: \nL86: ^{3}Unidad de Investigación en Enfermedades Metabólicas, Instituto Nacional de Nutrición Salvador Zubirán and Instituto Tecnológico y de Estudios Superiores de Monterrey Tec Salud, Ciudad de México, Mexico\nL87: \nL88: ^{4}Facultad de Enfermería y Nutriología, Universidad Autónoma de Chihuahua, Chihuahua, Mexico\nL89: ^{5}Centro Dermatológico Dr Ladislao de la Pascua, Ciudad de México, Mexico\nL90: \nL91: ^{6}Departamento de Endocrinología y Metabolismo, Instituto Nacional de Ciencias Médicas y Nutrición Salvador Zubirán, Ciudad de México, Mexico\nL92: ### Corresponding Author:\nL93: \nL94: Paloma Almeda-Valdes, MD, MSc, PhD\nL95: \nL96: Unidad de Investigación en Enfermedades Metabólicas\nL97: \nL98: Instituto Nacional de Nutrición Salvador Zubirán and Instituto Tecnológico y de Estudios Superiores de Monterrey Tec Salud\nL99: \nL100: Vasco de Quiroga #15\nL101: \nL102: Tlalpan\nL103: \nL104: Ciudad de México, 14080\nL105: \nL106: Mexico\nL107: \nL108: Phone: 52 54870900 ext 6322\nL109: \nL110: Email: palomaalmeda@yahoo.com\nL111: ### Abstract\nL112: \nL113: Background: The System Usability Scale (SUS) is a common metric used to assess the usability of a system, and it was initially developed in English. The implementation of electronic systems for clinical counseling (eHealth and mobile health) is increasing worldwide. Therefore, tools are needed to evaluate these applications in the languages and regional contexts in which the electronic tools are developed.\nL114: Objective: This study aims to translate, culturally adapt, and validate the original English version of the SUS into a Spanish version.\nL115: Methods: The translation process included forward and backward translation. Forward translations were made by 2 native Spanish speakers who spoke English as their second language, and a backward translation was made by a native English speaker. The Spanish SUS questionnaire was validated by 10 experts in mobile app development. The face validity of the questionnaire was tested with 10 mobile phone users, and the reliability testing was conducted among 88 electronic application users.\nL116: Results: The content validity index of the new Spanish SUS was good, as indicated by a rating of 0.92 for the relevance of the items. The questionnaire was easy to understand, based on a face validity index of 0.94. The Cronbach α was .812 (95% CI 0.748-0.866; P<.001).\nL117: \nL118: Conclusions: The new Spanish SUS questionnaire is a valid and reliable tool to assess the usability of electronic tools among Spanish-speaking users.\nL119: \nL120: JMIR Hum Factors 2020;7(4):e21161\nL121: 【75†doi:10.2196/21161†doi.org】\nL122: ### Keywords\nL123: \nL124: 【76†mHealth】; 【77†usability】; 【78†validation】; 【79†System Usability Scale】; 【80†Spanish】\nL125: ### Introduction\nL126: The mobile health (mHealth) concept encompasses clinical and public health practices that incorporate mobile devices, such as smartphones, tablets, personal digital assistants, and patient monitoring devices [【81†1】]. According to estimates, there are more than 325,000 mHealth apps for the most popular mobile platforms, iOS and Android [【82†2】].\nL127: Categories of mHealth products encompass monitoring, treatment, diagnosis, health professional support, well-being, health surveillance support, and health care administration [【83†3】]. Although download indexes and apps in the market have increased in the last 5 years, clinicians, researchers, and patients remain skeptical about the reliability of the data generated [【84†4】].\nL128: These limitations have led to a lack of knowledge regarding the efficiency, efficacy, and safety associated with mobile app utilization in clinical practice. Furthermore, health organizations recommend making assessments before software implementation to ensure safety and accurate data quality [【85†5】].\nL129: Usability is an essential part of software development and is commonly evaluated through questionnaires [【84†4】,【86†6】]. Questionnaires reflect users’ opinions and have the advantages of low cost, easy execution, and lack of necessary test equipment. Usability can be defined as the extent to which a product can be used by specific users to achieve specified goals effectively and efficiently while providing user satisfaction in a specific context of use (user technology interface) [【87†7】].\nL130: Due to the high demand for mHealth apps, usability evaluations are insufficient. Therefore, it is necessary to implement a usability metric that is context driven and standardized to efficiently assess clinically related software. There is no usability questionnaire specifically designed for mHealth apps. Previous studies have investigated usability models for mobile apps and have also modified existing usability questionnaires for use in mobile app usability studies [【88†8】].\nL131: The System Usability Scale (SUS) proposed by Brooke [【89†9】] in 1986 is a widely used questionnaire to assess the usability of a system, such as standard operating system–based software interfaces, webpages, and mobile apps. It has been implemented in several mHealth fields, including mental health (n=12), cancer (n=10), nutrition (n=10), pediatrics (n=9), diabetes (n=9), telemedicine (n=8), cardiovascular disease (n=6), HIV (n=4), sanitary information systems (n=4), and smoking (n=4) [【82†2】].\nL132: The SUS questionnaire has been translated into several languages, such as Portuguese [【90†10】], Indonesian [【91†11】], and more recently, Malay [【92†12】]. All translated versions have shown similar internal reliability compared with the original English version. Although there is a Spanish version [【93†13】], there is no evidence of the validity and reliability process.\nL133: Therefore, it is necessary to have a Spanish version of the SUS that documents the validation process in order to guarantee the quality of the resulting questionnaire. The objective of this study is to develop and validate a Spanish version of the original English SUS, guaranteeing conceptual, semantic, and contextual equivalence between both questionnaires.\nL134: ### Methods\nL135: #### SUS Scale\nL136: The SUS scale is a 10-item questionnaire scored on a 5-point Likert-type scale from 1 (strongly disagree) to 5 (strongly agree). Its advantages include versatility, simplicity, low cost, accuracy, and validity. Its reliability (Cronbach α=.85) has been reported [【91†11】,【92†12】,【94†14】-【95†16】]. The questionnaire is designed to be answered after the user’s interaction with the system. It is arranged to alternate between positive and negative statements to avoid habitual bias from the respondent.\nL137: The score contribution for the odd items (the positive statements) is the scale position minus 1 and the contribution for the even items (the negative statements) is 5 minus the scale position. The overall score is calculated from the sum of all item scores multiplied by 2.5, with the overall score ranging from 0 to 100. A system with a score above 85 is considered to have excellent usability, whereas a system with a score between 68 and 84 is considered to have good usability.\nL138: #### Translation\nL139: The original SUS questionnaire was translated into a new Spanish version using the methodology described by Ortiz-Gutiérrez and Cruz-Avelar [【96†17】], following the international guidelines proposed by the World Health Organization [【85†5】] to ensure the semantic equivalence, quality, and consistency of meaning with the original version.\nL140: The methodology included 9 steps: (1) preparation, (2) forward translation, (3) synthesis, (4) back translation, (5) review of the back translation, (6) revision of the target language phrasing, (7) harmonization, (8) piloting, and (9) completion.\nL141: First, for the preparation, we evaluated the measurement properties of the original tool, identifying differences and similarities among them. The author’s permission was requested to work with the scale.\nL142: Second, to achieve the forward translation, the original version of the SUS was translated into Spanish by 2 independent translators with an adequate understanding of the source language: one individual had a master’s deg